### A3. Research gaps

| Code | Gap (1–2 sentences) | Evidence (# in A2) | How our project addresses it |
|---|---|---|---|
| G1 | OCT/OCTA models are rarely externally validated, and performance drops when the population or device changes. | #4: only 13/53 studies externally validated; #5: ACC 87.3% → 70.8%; #3: external AUROC lower than primary; #10: large performance variation in real-world use | Partially: FairVision ↔ OLIVES encoder transfer (different devices and populations) with from-scratch and ImageNet controls (week 12); try Kermany/Duke in week 13, otherwise report as a limitation. The team does **not** have external data for the exact VTDR task. |
| G2 | Small single-center datasets yield near-perfect results without confidence intervals. | #8: AUC ≈ 0.99 on 72 eyes; whereas #7 (875 eyes) and #9 (402 eyes) reach only AUC 0.84–0.89 | Use FairVision (10,000 patients, test 3,000); patient-level split; report mean ± SD over ≥ 3 seeds and patient-level bootstrap CIs; for OLIVES (87 patients) state the wide CI explicitly (~±5 AUC points). |
| G3 | Fairness across demographic groups is almost never reported. | #1 is the only paper in A2 (and 1/40 in the matrix) reporting AUC by race, sex and ethnicity; #10 shows performance varying by site | Report per-group AUC, ES-AUC, PSD; reproduce and test FIS (B1, B2); bootstrap CIs for small groups (Asian: 233 test patients). |
| G4 | Reference labels are often not independent of, or are tied to, data collection, so AUC may measure "data source" rather than disease. | #1: VTDR labels from ICD codes; #2: DR/DME label is the trial name. Our finding: BCVA + CST alone reach AUC 0.986 at the first OLIVES visit (B1) | Add image-free baselines (logistic regression on BCVA, CST, biomarkers, intensity statistics); report first and last visits separately; propose shifting the OLIVES focus to biomarker detection. |
| G5 | Papers report only discrimination (AUC, ACC); calibration and Brier score are largely absent. | None of the 12 papers in A2 reports calibration or a Brier score in its main results (checked on full-text reading) | Add calibration curve, calibration-in-the-large, slope, Brier score with CIs (weeks 10–13). **The team has not done this yet either** (C4). |

### A4. PROBAST+AI appraisal of the most important paper in A2

Paper #1 (Luo et al., FairVision) · Reason: it is the source of the main dataset (Branch A), of the baseline the team reproduces (AUC 92.22), and of the FIS method the team proposes at Milestone 3. The *performance evaluation* part is appraised because the team uses these results as its reference.

| Domain | Risk of bias (low / high / unclear) | Justification (quote or page in the paper) |
|---|---|---|
| Participants and data sources | High (applicability); unclear (risk of bias) | All 30,000 patients from one health system (Harvard Ophthalmology AI Lab, USA), retrospective, one scan per patient; the parts read do not state inclusion/exclusion criteria, time period or acquisition device. Demographics: White 78.6%, Black 13.7%, Asian 7.7%. [TO FILL page number: dataset description section in `Havard_Fair_Vision.pdf`] |
| Predictors | Unclear | Inputs are 3D OCT 128×200×200 and SLO 200×200, clearly defined; but device, acquisition protocol and preprocessing are not reported, so it is unknown whether predictors were collected the same way for all patients and blinded to the outcome. |
| Outcome | High | VTDR (severe NPDR or PDR) derived from ICD codes in the records, not from independent graders using a standard scale; the paper itself notes ICD labels may be noisy. ICD codes are entered by clinicians after viewing images (possibly including this OCT), so the label may not be independent of the predictors; the timing of coding relative to the scan is not reported. |
| Analysis | High | Fixed 6,000/1,000/3,000 split, one sample per patient (no patient-level leakage — a strength). However: only AUC and fairness metrics, no calibration or Brier score; uncertainty only as SD across seeds, no patient-level CI; small minority groups (Asian test ≈ 233) without CIs; no external validation. FIS details (OT estimation, weight scale) are insufficient for reproduction (B3). |

Applicability to our problem: usable as a reproduction benchmark on the same dataset. Not usable to draw conclusions for Vietnamese patients (different population, device, ICD labels) or for real-world screening.

## B. Results and timeline

### B1. Current results

Branch A: fixed FairVision-DR test set, n = 3,000 patients (8.7% positive). Branch B: OLIVES, 5 patient-level folds (87 patients, 96 eyes, 192 volumes from first + last visits). Mean ± SD is across seeds (SD divided by n).

| Model | Evaluation set (split, n) | Primary metric (value, 95% CI if available) | Secondary metrics | Source (file / notebook / W&B) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Baseline 1 (Milestone 2) — A: r3d_18 Kinetics-400, 3D OCT 64×128×128, 5 epochs | FairVision test, fixed split, n = 3,000; 3 seeds (0, 1, 2) | AUC 94.98 ± 0.19. No patient-level CI for overall AUC yet (in progress, week 10) | Race ES-AUC 87.61 ± 0.77; Mean PSD 3.54 ± 0.46; Black AUC 89.2 ± 0.6. Seed 0, Youden threshold 0.108 chosen on val: sensitivity 90.4%, specificity 89.4%, bAcc 89.9% | `results/fairvision/r3d_e5/seed{0,1,2}/results.json`; `results/fairvision/compare_fis_vs_baseline.csv`; command in `src/train.py` |
| Baseline 2 (Milestone 2) — A: ViT-B MAE, 2D SLO | FairVision test, n = 3,000; 5 seeds | AUC 83.16 ± 0.36 | Race ES-AUC 74.10 ± 0.92 | `results/fairvision/vit_mae_slo/seed0..4/results.json` |
| Baseline 3 — B: image-free logistic regression (BCVA + CST) | OLIVES, 5 patient-level folds, 192 volumes | AUC 0.860 [0.808; 0.908] (patient-level bootstrap CI) | bAcc 79.5%; AUC first visit 0.986, last visit 0.776 | `results/olives/olives_cv_results/` (logistic baseline) |
| Proposed model (Milestone 3) — A: r3d_18 + FIS (race attribute, c = 0.5; team's implementation) | FairVision test, n = 3,000; 3 seeds | AUC 95.35 ± 0.17; paired difference vs baseline +0.28/+0.39/+0.44 (paired t, p ≈ 0.016). Test-sample bootstrap (seeds 1, 2): most difference CIs include 0 | Race ES-AUC 89.62 ± 0.56 (p ≈ 0.10); Mean PSD 2.52 ± 0.22 (p ≈ 0.16); Black AUC 91.0 ± 0.1 | `results/fairvision/fis_r3d/seed{0,1,2}/results.json`; `results/fairvision/compare_fis_vs_baseline.csv`; `src/models.py` (FISLoss) |
| Proposed model (Milestone 3) — B: ImageNet ResNet-18 on B-scans, mean probability over 49 B-scans/volume | OLIVES, 5 patient-level folds, 192 volumes; 5 seeds | Volume AUC 90.6 ± 0.9; pooled over 5 seeds: 91.0 [86.0; 95.5] (patient-level bootstrap CI) | bAcc@0.5 82.8 ± 1.7; AUC first visit 97.6 ± 1.0; last visit 82.8 ± 1.2 | `src/olives_cv.py`; `results/olives/olives_cv_results/` |

Interpretation: on OLIVES, the image model (first-visit AUC 97.6) **does not outperform** the image-free BCVA + CST baseline (98.6); at the last visit it is on par with BCVA + CST + biomarkers (≈ 83). FIS is beneficial in the same direction across all 3 seeds, but the statistical evidence is not yet sufficient.

### B2. Ablation (Milestone 3)

Full model, Branch A: r3d_18 Kinetics + FIS (AUC 95.35). Full model, Branch B: ImageNet ResNet-18, patient-level split (AUC 90.6).

| Configuration (component removed / changed) | Primary metric | Difference vs full model | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| A: remove FIS (= baseline 1), 3 seeds | AUC 94.98 ± 0.19; ES-AUC 87.61 | AUC −0.37; race ES-AUC −2.01; Mean PSD +1.02 | `results/fairvision/compare_fis_vs_baseline.csv` |
| A: remove FIS and Kinetics pretraining, 1 seed | AUC 93.51 | −1.84 vs full; vs baseline with the same seed a drop of ≈ 1.4 (test-sample bootstrap CI 0.6–2.3). Only 1 seed, 5 epochs | `results/fairvision/r3d_scratch/seed0/results.json` |
| A: replace 3D OCT with 2D SLO (ViT-B MAE), 5 seeds | AUC 83.16 ± 0.36 | −12.19 | `results/fairvision/vit_mae_slo/` |
| B: remove ImageNet pretraining, 1 seed | Volume AUC 79.0; bAcc 67.7 | −11.6 AUC | `results/olives/olives_cv_results/` (no-pretrain run) |
| B: patient-level → eye-level split, 3 fold partitions | Volume AUC higher or equal in all 3 partitions, mean +0.9 (95% CI −1.0; +2.8); bAcc inconsistent (−2.0; +0.35; +2.45) | +0.9 (suggests minor leakage, not conclusive) | `results/olives/olives_eyefold_results/`, `results/olives/olives_partitions_results/` |
| B: replace images with 5 B-scan intensity statistics (logistic regression) | AUC 0.787 (B-scan level) | Not directly comparable (B-scan level, not volume) | `results/olives/olives_cv_results/` |

Not yet done: Branch A preprocessing ablation (percentile clipping, Kinetics-style normalization, depth 128) to explain the remaining +1.3-point gap vs the paper; AUC under fp16 inference.

### B3. Comparison with the literature

- **vs #1 (FairVision, same dataset, same fixed test set):** ours 94.98 ± 0.19 vs 92.22 ± 0.53 (3D OCT); FIS 95.35 vs 93.37. Not a fully fair comparison because: (1) different architecture (Kinetics-pretrained r3d_18 vs the paper's 3D ResNet); (2) we downsample volumes to 64×128×128 and train only 5 epochs (paper: original size, 50 epochs, 5 seeds); (3) the paper does not describe preprocessing; (4) FIS is the team's interpretation, since the paper does not specify OT estimation and weight scale; (5) we have not verified that our split matches the paper's. Pretraining explains about half of the +2.8 gap (B2); the rest is unexplained. For SLO we are 2.06 points below the paper (≈ 6 × the across-seed SD), cause not yet isolated.
- **vs #2 (OLIVES, same dataset):** our ResNet-18 bAcc 82.8 ± 1.7 vs 70.15 ± 4.69 in the paper. Not directly comparable: different split (5 patient-level folds vs eye-level), different unit of evaluation (volume vs B-scan), possibly different preprocessing and hyperparameters (paper appendix unavailable). More importantly, our BCVA + CST baseline reaches AUC 0.986 at the first visit, so DR/DME numbers from both the paper and the team may mainly reflect differences between the two trials (G4).
- **vs #3 (Tang 2021):** AUROC 0.937–0.965 for DME detection on OCT. Different task (DME vs VTDR), different labels (graders vs ICD codes), different devices and populations, and #3 has external validation while we only have an internal test set. Used only to gauge feasibility, not for ranking.

