|---|---|---|
| Source, version, download date; checksum of the files used for training | **FairVision-DR:** Harvard Ophthalmology AI Lab, https://ophai.hms.harvard.edu/datasets/harvard-fairvision30k; DR folder (~40–52 GB); version and download date [TO FILL]; **no checksum yet** for `dataset.zip` and the `.npy` caches (only a `check()` function verifying sample count and file size). **OLIVES:** Zenodo, DOI 10.5281/zenodo.7105232, version v2 (09/06/2022); published MD5: `OLIVES.zip` e08f4198aa919d6900bd30525c137933, `OLIVES_Dataset_Labels.zip` e0b14b14f457e4abcafafdd60b8d3952; the team has not yet verified the MD5 locally | Dataset page; Zenodo page; `data/CHECKSUMS.sha256` to be added (sha256 of the original zips, `slo_224/*.npy`, `3d_d64_s128/*.npy`, `olives_bscan224.npy`) |
| License / DUA, basis for permitted use | FairVision: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 (non-commercial, no redistribution of derivatives), so the downsampled cache is kept Private on Kaggle and **no images are put in the repository**. OLIVES: CC BY 4.0 (Zenodo page) | FairVision dataset card; Zenodo page |
| Datasheet: population, inclusion/exclusion criteria, variable definitions and units, limitations | A datasheet-equivalent description exists in the summary report (section 2: source, ethics, devices, demographics, label definitions, variable coding, limitations). Not yet in the Gebru template; missing FairVision inclusion/exclusion criteria (not stated in the paper) and CST units (not given in the OLIVES paper) | `report/` [TO FILL: file name after adding the report to the repository] |
| No patient identifiers | Both datasets are de-identified at the source (FairVision: IRB, de-identified; OLIVES: no identifying information under HIPAA). The repository contains only code, anonymized ID manifests and aggregate results, no images | Original papers #1, #2; `.gitignore` |

### C2. Reporting guideline

- Research question: From a 3D OCT volume (and SLO image) acquired at one visit, how well can a deep learning model distinguish people with vision-threatening DR (VTDR: severe NPDR or PDR) from those without, and is performance consistent across race, sex and ethnicity groups? Branch B: from OCT B-scans, identify DR/DME and biomarkers.
- Appropriate reporting guideline (TRIPOD+AI / STARD-AI / DECIDE-AI) and rationale (Appendix §3.2): **TRIPOD+AI**, because the team *develops and internally validates* a diagnostic prediction model (training, architecture comparison, FIS, threshold selection), rather than evaluating the accuracy of a locked AI test against a reference standard (STARD-AI) or evaluating clinical use of the tool by clinicians (DECIDE-AI). STARD-AI will additionally be consulted for sensitivity/specificity at the chosen threshold.
- Number of checklist items met / total; missing items: **27/27** items completed in `report/tripod_ai_checklist.md`. Analyses that the checklist reports as not yet done in the results, and that are scheduled in B4: calibration and Brier score, patient-level CI for the Branch A primary metric, sample size calculation.
- Outcome, label, prediction time T0, variables available at T0:
  - Outcome (A): binary VTDR, `dr_subtype` ∈ {severe.npdr, pdr} = 1; {no.dr.diagnosis, not.in.icd.table, mild.npdr, moderate.npdr} = 0. Label source: ICD codes in the records. `dr_class` (a different 3-class label) is not used.
  - Outcome (B): DR (PRIME) or DME (TREX-DME), assigned by trial; binary per-B-scan biomarkers assigned by graders blinded to clinical information.
  - T0: time of OCT acquisition at the visit.
  - Variables at T0: (A) 3D OCT volume 128×200×200, SLO image 200×200. Race, sex and ethnicity are available at T0 but **used only for fairness evaluation and FIS loss weighting during training**, not as inference inputs. (B) 49 B-scans, BCVA and CST measured at the same visit.
  - Point to clarify: the timing of ICD coding relative to the scan is not reported by the dataset, so it is not certain that the label reflects the status *at* T0.

### C3. PROBAST+AI — self-appraisal of the team's project

| Domain | Risk of bias (low / high / unclear) | Evidence in the repository (file, line) | Actions to reduce risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| Participants and data sources | A: unclear (dataset inclusion/exclusion criteria not published). B: high — 87 patients from two different trials, not a screening population | `src/data.py` (reads `data_summary_dr.csv`, `use` column for the split) [line: fill after commit]; `results/olives/manifest_olives_firstlast.csv` | State population and limitations in the datasheet; do not generalize to community screening or Vietnamese patients |
| Predictors | Low–unclear. Min-max preprocessing **per image/volume** and ImageNet normalization use constants, so nothing is fitted on the full dataset; FairVision acquisition device unknown | `src/cache_fv_zip.py` (min-max, downsampling); `src/data.py` (normalization) [line: fill] | Shortcut-learning check: metadata-only/intensity-only models (done for OLIVES: AUC 0.787 at B-scan level; not yet for FairVision) |
| Outcome (note incorporation bias) | **High.** A: ICD codes entered by clinicians after viewing images, possibly this very OCT → label not independent of the input. B: DR/DME label = trial name; if CST/BCVA were enrollment criteria for TREX-DME, the BCVA + CST baseline (AUC 0.986 at first visit) suffers incorporation bias, and the image model may learn "thickness/edema" rather than "DR vs DME" (hypothesis, not yet tested) | `src/data.py` (`dr_subtype` → label mapping) [line: fill]; `src/olives_cv.py` (label from trial name) [line: fill] | Read Summaries_DR_DME_Studies to confirm enrollment criteria; report first/last visits separately; shift to biomarkers (labeled by blinded graders); state the ICD label limitation |
| Analysis (patient-level split, fitting on train only, sample size, missing data) | **High.** Strengths: A one sample per patient, predefined split; B 5 patient-level folds with an assert (8/9 patients with both eyes would be split under an eye-level split); epoch and threshold chosen on val. Weaknesses: (1) no calibration or Brier score yet; (2) no patient-level CI for Branch A overall AUC yet; (3) many configurations reported on test → risk of test-based model selection; (4) small groups (16 positive Asian cases in test); (5) OLIVES 87 patients, val-selected threshold ranges 0.002–0.88; (6) no sample size calculation | `src/train.py` (epoch selected by val AUC) [line: fill]; `src/utils.py` (Youden on val) [line: fill]; `src/olives_cv.py` (assert no patient overlap between folds) [line: fill] | Add calibration (curve, in-the-large, slope) + Brier score with bootstrap CIs; lock the configuration before looking at test and record which configuration was chosen on val; per-group bootstrap CIs; use AUC and bAcc@0.5 as primary for OLIVES |

Applicability (population, intended use): adults with diabetes imaged with OCT at one US health system (A) and DR/DME trial patients at one clinic in Texas (B). The model is at the internal validation stage and is not yet applicable to screening in Vietnam: different population (Asian only 7.7%), different OCT devices, ICD labels, no calibration and no external validation yet.

